<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="u5-optimization-training" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Eigenvalues and second-order optimization</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Eigenvectors identify directions that a square matrix maps back to the same line. Diagonalization collects enough of these directions into a coordinate system in which the matrix acts by separate scalings. Symmetric matrices admit orthonormal eigenvector coordinates, which also simplify quadratic forms. We then apply these tools to Hessians and revisit least squares through projection, gradients, and curvature.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Big questions.</em> Which directions make a matrix simple? How do those directions describe quadratic forms and the local shape of a function? How do projection, zero gradient, and curvature describe the same least-squares problem?
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Learning outcomes.</em> By the end of this unit, students should be able to:
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Core outcomes.</em>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>U5-LO1.</em> Compute eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and eigenspaces, and interpret eigenvectors as directions whose lines are preserved by a square matrix.</li>
      <li>
        <em>U5-LO2.</em> Determine whether a matrix is diagonalizable, construct a diagonalization
        <me>A=PDP^{-1}</me>
        when possible, and use it to simplify matrix actions and powers.
      </li>
      <li><em>U5-LO3.</em> Orthogonally diagonalize symmetric matrices and interpret the spectral theorem as an orthonormal change of coordinates.</li>
      <li><em>U5-LO4.</em> Analyze quadratic forms in principal-axis coordinates and classify symmetric matrices as positive definite, negative definite, or indefinite.</li>
      <li><em>U5-LO5.</em> Use quadratic approximations, Hessian eigenvalues, contour plots, and the two-variable discriminant shortcut to classify critical points.</li>
      <li><em>U5-LO6.</em> Derive and interpret least squares through residual orthogonality, zero gradient, Hessian curvature, global minimality, and uniqueness.</li>
      <li>
        <em>U5-LO7.</em> Construct a design matrix from fixed nonlinear features, write the squared-error loss
        <me>L(\mathbf c)=\|A\mathbf c-\mathbf y\|^2,</me>
        and explain why fitting only <m>\mathbf c</m> is a least-squares problem.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Toolbox skills.</em> Compute eigenvalues, eigenvectors, eigenspaces, diagonalizations, matrix powers, orthogonal diagonalizations, quadratic forms, definiteness, Hessian eigenvalue classifications, normal equations, residual checks, and fixed-feature design matrices.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Application and computation outcomes.</em> Interpret Lab U5 <c>[U3-LO6, U3-LO9, U5-LO5, U5-LO6, U5-LO7 | C+M+T | Core]</c> by reading gradient-descent and final-layer updates as Unit 3 review, then reading Hessian eigenvalues, least-squares solves, residual checks, fixed-feature design matrices, and fitted coefficients as Unit 5 applications.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="sec-u5-eigenvalues-and-eigenvectors.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u5-diagonalization.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u5-symmetric-diagonalization.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u5-quadratic-forms-definiteness.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-u5-second-derivative-test-saddle-points.ptx" />
  <section xml:id="sec-least-squares-redux">
    <title>Least squares: three viewpoints</title>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>The squared-residual loss</title>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="sec-u4-least-squares-as-projection" text="custom">the Unit 4 least-squares section</xref>, least squares was a <xref ref="thm-6-2projection" text="custom">projection problem</xref>. Given a matrix <m>A</m> and a target vector <m>\mathbf b</m>, a <xref ref="sec-least-squares-projection" text="custom">least-squares coefficient vector</xref> <m>\widehat{\mathbf x}</m> makes <m>A\widehat{\mathbf x}</m> as close as possible to <m>\mathbf b</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We now study the same problem through the squared-residual loss
        <me>
          L(\mathbf x)=\|A\mathbf x-\mathbf b\|^2.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Squaring the <xref ref="sec-least-squares-projection" text="custom">residual</xref> norm does not change its minimizers, and the squared expression is easier to differentiate. This section connects three viewpoints:
        <me>
          \text{residual orthogonality}
          \longleftrightarrow
          \text{zero gradient}
          \longleftrightarrow
          \text{curvature from }A^TA.
        </me>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u5-derivative-identities-least-squares">
      <title>Derivative identities for least squares</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute the following derivatives.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>For a fixed vector <m>\mathbf d</m>, let</p>
            <me>
              g(\mathbf x)=\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf x.
            </me>
            <p>Compute <m>\nabla g(\mathbf x)</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For a fixed <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, let</p>
            <me>
              q(\mathbf x)=\mathbf x^TA\mathbf x.
            </me>
            <p>Compute <m>\nabla q(\mathbf x)</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For a fixed <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>C</m>, let</p>
            <me>
              F(\mathbf x)=C\mathbf x.
            </me>
            <p>Compute <m>J_F(\mathbf x)</m>.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Expanding the first function gives
          <me>
            g(\mathbf x)=d_1x_1+\cdots+d_nx_n.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \frac{\partial g}{\partial x_i}=d_i,
            \qquad
            \nabla g(\mathbf x)=\mathbf d.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For the quadratic function,
          <me>
            q(\mathbf x)
            =
            \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij}x_ix_j.
          </me>
          For <m>1\leq k\leq n</m>,
          <me>
            \frac{\partial q}{\partial x_k}
            =
            \sum_{j=1}^n A_{kj}x_j
            +
            \sum_{i=1}^n A_{ik}x_i.
          </me>
          This is the <m>k</m>-th entry of
          <me>
            (A+A^T)\mathbf x.
          </me>
          Hence
          <me>
            \nabla q(\mathbf x)=(A+A^T)\mathbf x.
          </me>
          In particular, if <m>A</m> is symmetric, then
          <me>
            \nabla q(\mathbf x)=2A\mathbf x.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, the <m>i</m>-th component of <m>F</m> is
          <me>
            F_i(\mathbf x)=C_{i1}x_1+\cdots+C_{in}x_n.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            \frac{\partial F_i}{\partial x_j}=C_{ij},
          </me>
          and therefore
          <me>
            J_F(\mathbf x)=C.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
      <title>Gradient zero gives the normal equations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix and let <m>\mathbf b\in\mathbb R^m</m>. Define
          <me>
            L(\mathbf x)=\|A\mathbf x-\mathbf b\|^2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Expand <m>L(\mathbf x)</m> as a quadratic function of <m>\mathbf x</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>\nabla L(\mathbf x)</m>.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>Show that every critical point satisfies</p>
            <me>
              A^TA\mathbf x=A^T\mathbf b.
            </me>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>If</p>
            <me>
              \mathbf r=\mathbf b-A\widehat{\mathbf x},
            </me>
            <p>show that the normal equations are equivalent to</p>
            <me>
              A^T\mathbf r=\mathbf0.
            </me>
            <p>How was this condition interpreted in <xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality" text="custom">the Unit 4 normal-equations theorem</xref>?</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We expand
          <md>
            <mrow>
              L(\mathbf x)
              \amp=(A\mathbf x-\mathbf b)^T(A\mathbf x-\mathbf b)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp=\mathbf x^TA^TA\mathbf x
              -2(A^T\mathbf b)^T\mathbf x
              +\mathbf b^T\mathbf b.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>A^TA</m> is symmetric, <xref ref="act-u5-derivative-identities-least-squares" text="custom">the preceding activity</xref> gives
          <me>
            \nabla L(\mathbf x)
            =
            2A^TA\mathbf x-2A^T\mathbf b
            =
            2A^T(A\mathbf x-\mathbf b).
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \nabla L(\mathbf x)=\mathbf0
          </me>
          exactly when
          <me>
            A^TA\mathbf x=A^T\mathbf b.
          </me>
          These are the normal equations.
        </p>
        <p>
          If
          <me>
            \mathbf r=\mathbf b-A\widehat{\mathbf x},
          </me>
          then
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf r
            =
            A^T\mathbf b-A^TA\widehat{\mathbf x}.
          </me>
          Thus the normal equations are equivalent to
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf r=\mathbf0.
          </me>
          In Unit 4, this says that the residual is orthogonal to every column of <m>A</m>, or equivalently,
          <me>
            \mathbf r\perp\operatorname{col}(A).
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u5-same-normal-equations-in-code">
      <title>The same condition in code</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Consider the code</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[xhat = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]
r = b - A @ xhat
A.T @ r
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What does <c>xhat</c> represent?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does <c>r</c> represent?</p></li>
          <li><p>What condition is checked by <c>A.T @ r</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>How do the projection and gradient viewpoints interpret the same output?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO6, U4-LO4 | C+T | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The vector <c>xhat</c> is a least-squares coefficient vector. The vector <c>r</c> is the residual
          <me>
            \mathbf r=\mathbf b-A\widehat{\mathbf x}.
          </me>
          The expression <c>A.T @ r</c> checks numerically whether
          <me>
            A^T\mathbf r=\mathbf0.
          </me>
          Unit 4 interprets this condition as residual orthogonality. In the current section,
          <me>
            \nabla L(\widehat{\mathbf x})
            =
            2A^T(A\widehat{\mathbf x}-\mathbf b)
            =
            -2A^T\mathbf r,
          </me>
          so the same calculation also checks whether the gradient is zero.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity>
      <title>Curvature, global minimality, and uniqueness</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>\widehat{\mathbf x}</m> satisfies the normal equations, and define
          <me>
            \mathbf r=\mathbf b-A\widehat{\mathbf x}.
          </me>
          Let <m>\mathbf z\in\mathbb R^n</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute the Hessian <m>H_L(\mathbf x)</m>.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>Show that</p>
            <me>
              \mathbf z^TA^TA\mathbf z=\|A\mathbf z\|^2.
            </me>
            <p>What does this say about the curvature of <m>L</m>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Use <m>A^T\mathbf r=\mathbf0</m> to prove the exact identity</p>
            <me>
              L(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
              =
              L(\widehat{\mathbf x})+\|A\mathbf z\|^2.
            </me>
          </li>
          <li><p>Explain why <m>\widehat{\mathbf x}</m> is a global minimizer.</p></li>
          <li><p>Suppose the <xref ref="thm-5-3-rank-nullity" text="custom">columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent</xref>. Explain why the minimizer is unique.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>Suppose <m>\mathbf z\neq\mathbf0</m> lies in <m>\operatorname{null}(A)</m>. Compare</p>
            <me>
              A(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
              \quad\text{and}\quad
              A\widehat{\mathbf x},
            </me>
            <p>and compare their loss values.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From
          <me>
            \nabla L(\mathbf x)=2A^T(A\mathbf x-\mathbf b),
          </me>
          we obtain
          <me>
            H_L(\mathbf x)=2A^TA.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For every <m>\mathbf z</m>,
          <me>
            \mathbf z^TA^TA\mathbf z
            =
            (A\mathbf z)^T(A\mathbf z)
            =
            \|A\mathbf z\|^2
            \geq0.
          </me>
          Thus the loss has nonnegative quadratic curvature in every direction. A direction is flat exactly when
          <me>
            A\mathbf z=\mathbf0.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since
          <me>
            A(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)-\mathbf b
            =
            A\mathbf z-\mathbf r,
          </me>
          we have
          <md>
            <mrow>
              L(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
              \amp=
              \|A\mathbf z-\mathbf r\|^2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp=
              \|A\mathbf z\|^2
              -2\mathbf r^TA\mathbf z
              +\|\mathbf r\|^2.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          But
          <me>
            \mathbf r^TA\mathbf z
            =
            (A^T\mathbf r)^T\mathbf z
            =
            0,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            L(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
            =
            \|\mathbf r\|^2+\|A\mathbf z\|^2
            =
            L(\widehat{\mathbf x})+\|A\mathbf z\|^2.
          </me>
          Since <m>\|A\mathbf z\|^2\geq0</m>, no other coefficient vector has smaller loss. Therefore <m>\widehat{\mathbf x}</m> is a global minimizer.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, then
          <me>
            A\mathbf z=\mathbf0
          </me>
          implies <m>\mathbf z=\mathbf0</m>. The displayed identity is then strict for every nonzero <m>\mathbf z</m>, so the minimizer is unique. Equivalently, <m>A^TA</m> is invertible and
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf x}
            =
            (A^TA)^{-1}A^T\mathbf b.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>\mathbf z\neq\mathbf0</m> lies in <m>\operatorname{null}(A)</m>, then
          <me>
            A(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
            =
            A\widehat{\mathbf x},
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            L(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
            =
            L(\widehat{\mathbf x}).
          </me>
          Thus the fitted vector remains the same, but the coefficient vector is not unique.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <warning>
      <p>
        The <xref ref="thm-u5-general-second-derivative-test" text="custom">second derivative test</xref> is a local test. The exact identity
        <me>
          L(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
          =
          L(\widehat{\mathbf x})+\|A\mathbf z\|^2
        </me>
        gives the stronger global conclusion. Linear independence is needed for uniqueness of the coefficient vector, not for <xref ref="sec-u4-least-squares-as-projection" text="custom">the least-squares problem to have a minimizer</xref>.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>One equation, three viewpoints</title>
      <tabular>
        <col width="16%"/>
        <col width="32%"/>
        <col width="42%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Viewpoint</cell>
          <cell>Key calculation</cell>
          <cell>Interpretation</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Projection</cell>
          <cell><m>A^T(\mathbf b-A\widehat{\mathbf x})=\mathbf0</m></cell>
          <cell><p>The residual is orthogonal to <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Gradient</cell>
          <cell><m>\nabla L(\widehat{\mathbf x})=\mathbf0</m></cell>
          <cell><p>The least-squares coefficient vector is a critical point of the squared-residual loss.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Curvature</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><m>H_L=2A^TA</m> and</p>
            <p><m>L(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)-L(\widehat{\mathbf x})</m></p>
            <p><m>=\|A\mathbf z\|^2</m></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The critical point is a global minimizer; it is unique when <m>\operatorname{null}(A)=\{\mathbf0\}</m>.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>
        The normal equations are therefore one condition with three readings. Unit 4 supplied the geometry, the gradient gives the critical-point equation, and <m>A^TA</m> describes curvature and uniqueness.
      </p>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="sec-tiny-nn-training" text="custom">the fixed-feature section</xref>, the columns of a design matrix will be fixed nonlinear features. Training only their coefficient vector will then be exactly this least-squares problem.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-tiny-nn-training">
    <title>Least squares with fixed nonlinear features</title>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Fixed features and trained coefficients</title>
      <p>
        A model can be nonlinear in its input and still be linear in the parameters being fitted. Let
        <me>\phi_0,\phi_1,\ldots,\phi_p</me>
        be fixed scalar-valued feature functions. For a coefficient vector
        <me>
          \mathbf c=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          c_0\\
          c_1\\
          \vdots\\
          c_p
          \end{bmatrix},
        </me>
        define
        <me>
          N_{\mathbf c}(t)
          =
          c_0\phi_0(t)+c_1\phi_1(t)+\cdots+c_p\phi_p(t).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Equivalently, if
        <me>
          \boldsymbol{\phi}(t)
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \phi_0(t)\\
          \phi_1(t)\\
          \vdots\\
          \phi_p(t)
          \end{bmatrix},
        </me>
        then
        <me>N_{\mathbf c}(t)=\boldsymbol{\phi}(t)^T\mathbf c.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Given data
        <me>(t_1,y_1),\ldots,(t_m,y_m),</me>
        form the design matrix
        <me>
          A=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \phi_0(t_1)&amp;\phi_1(t_1)&amp;\cdots&amp;\phi_p(t_1)\\
          \phi_0(t_2)&amp;\phi_1(t_2)&amp;\cdots&amp;\phi_p(t_2)\\
          \vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots\\
          \phi_0(t_m)&amp;\phi_1(t_m)&amp;\cdots&amp;\phi_p(t_m)
          \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \mathbf y=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          y_1\\
          y_2\\
          \vdots\\
          y_m
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The prediction vector is
        <me>
          A\mathbf c=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          N_{\mathbf c}(t_1)\\
          N_{\mathbf c}(t_2)\\
          \vdots\\
          N_{\mathbf c}(t_m)
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        With squared-error loss,
        <me>
          L(\mathbf c)
          =
          \sum_{i=1}^m
          \bigl(N_{\mathbf c}(t_i)-y_i\bigr)^2
          =
          \|A\mathbf c-\mathbf y\|^2.
        </me>
        Thus minimizing <m>L</m> is exactly the least-squares problem from <xref ref="sec-least-squares-redux" text="custom">Least squares: three viewpoints</xref>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The feature functions determine the columns of <m>A</m>. They are fixed before the coefficients are fitted. Only <m>\mathbf c</m> is trained.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u5-fixed-features-trained-coefficients">
      <title>Fixed nonlinear features, trained coefficients</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>\sigma(t)=\tanh(t),</me>
          and define the fixed feature functions
          <me>
            \phi_0(t)=1,\qquad
            \phi_1(t)=\sigma(t),\qquad
            \phi_2(t)=\sigma(t-1),\qquad
            \phi_3(t)=\sigma(t+1).
          </me>
          The model is
          <me>
            N_{\mathbf c}(t)
            =
            c_0+c_1\sigma(t)+c_2\sigma(t-1)+c_3\sigma(t+1).
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For data <m>(t_i,y_i)</m>, define row <m>i</m> of <m>A</m> to be <m>\boldsymbol{\phi}(t_i)^T</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Write the feature vector <m>\boldsymbol{\phi}(t)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Write the design matrix <m>A</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Explain why the prediction vector is <m>A\mathbf c</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Write the squared-error loss <m>L(\mathbf c)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Which quantities are fixed, and which vector is trained?</p></li>
          <li><p>Is the model linear as a function of <m>t</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Is the model linear as a function of <m>\mathbf c</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why is fitting <m>\mathbf c</m> a least-squares problem?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO6, U5-LO7 | C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The feature vector is
          <me>
            \boldsymbol{\phi}(t)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            \tanh(t)\\
            \tanh(t-1)\\
            \tanh(t+1)
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            A=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1&amp;\tanh(t_1)&amp;\tanh(t_1-1)&amp;\tanh(t_1+1)\\
            1&amp;\tanh(t_2)&amp;\tanh(t_2-1)&amp;\tanh(t_2+1)\\
            \vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots\\
            1&amp;\tanh(t_m)&amp;\tanh(t_m-1)&amp;\tanh(t_m+1)
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Row <m>i</m> of <m>A</m> is <m>\boldsymbol{\phi}(t_i)^T</m>. Hence the <m>i</m>-th entry of <m>A\mathbf c</m> is
          <me>
            \boldsymbol{\phi}(t_i)^T\mathbf c
            =
            N_{\mathbf c}(t_i).
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The squared-error loss is
          <me>L(\mathbf c)=\|A\mathbf c-\mathbf y\|^2.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The inputs <m>t_i</m>, targets <m>y_i</m>, feature functions, and entries of <m>A</m> are fixed. The trained vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf c=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            c_0\\
            c_1\\
            c_2\\
            c_3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The model is nonlinear as a function of <m>t</m>, because the feature functions contain <m>\tanh</m>. It is linear as a function of <m>\mathbf c</m>, because the predictions have the form <m>A\mathbf c</m>. Therefore fitting <m>\mathbf c</m> by squared error is a least-squares problem.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      For
      <me>
        \mathbf t=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        -2\\-1\\0\\1\\2
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \mathbf y=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        4\\1\\0\\1\\4
        \end{bmatrix},
      </me>
      the design matrix has shape <m>5\times4</m>. The linked lab constructs this matrix, solves for <m>\mathbf c</m>, and compares the fitted values with the data.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>What <q>fixed hidden layer</q> means</title>
      <p>
        In machine-learning language, the feature vector <m>\boldsymbol{\phi}(t)</m> may be produced by a hidden layer. Calling the hidden layer fixed means that its internal weights, shifts, and nonlinear feature functions are not trained. Training only the final coefficients still gives the model
        <me>N_{\mathbf c}(t)=\boldsymbol{\phi}(t)^T\mathbf c,</me>
        so squared-error training is ordinary least squares.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="sec-u5-code-fixed-hidden-layer">
      <title>The same fixed-feature fit in code</title>
      <statement>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A = np.column_stack([
    np.ones_like(t),
    np.tanh(t),
    np.tanh(t - 1),
    np.tanh(t + 1)
])

c = np.linalg.lstsq(A, y, rcond=None)[0]
yhat = A @ c
loss = np.linalg.norm(yhat - y)**2
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What feature is stored in each column of <c>A</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which vector is trained?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does <c>yhat</c> contain?</p></li>
          <li><p>What quantity is stored in <c>loss</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why can <c>np.linalg.lstsq</c> be used?</p></li>
          <li><p>Is this code fitting the locations of the shifts inside <c>tanh</c>?</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO6, U5-LO7 | C+M+T | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The columns of <c>A</c> are the fixed feature vectors
          <me>
            \mathbf 1,\qquad
            \bigl[\tanh(t_i)\bigr]_{i=1}^m,\qquad
            \bigl[\tanh(t_i-1)\bigr]_{i=1}^m,\qquad
            \bigl[\tanh(t_i+1)\bigr]_{i=1}^m,
          </me>
          where each bracketed vector is evaluated entrywise at the data inputs. The trained vector is <m>\mathbf c</m>. The vector <c>yhat</c> contains the fitted values
          <me>A\mathbf c.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The variable <c>loss</c> stores
          <me>\|A\mathbf c-\mathbf y\|^2.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The command <c>np.linalg.lstsq</c> applies because <m>A</m> is fixed and the prediction vector is linear in <m>\mathbf c</m>. The shifts <m>0,1,-1</m> inside the feature functions are not being fitted.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <warning>
      <p>
        The least-squares reduction depends on the features being fixed. If the shifts <m>\beta_j</m> in features such as
        <me>\tanh(t-\beta_j)</me>
        are also trained, then the design matrix depends on the parameters:
        <me>A=A(\boldsymbol{\beta}).</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The loss
        <me>\|A(\boldsymbol{\beta})\mathbf c-\mathbf y\|^2</me>
        is no longer a quadratic function of all trained parameters. One least-squares solve does not train the whole model; <xref ref="sec-u3-critical-points-gradient-descent" text="custom">gradient descent</xref> and the <xref ref="sec-9-2-chain-rule-1" text="custom">chain rule</xref> from Unit 3 are then needed.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u5-apps-computation">
    <title>Applications and computation recap</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Application question.</em> How do spectral tools, Hessian tests, and least-squares calculations appear in short computations?
      </p>
      <p>
        A short Unit 5 computation should be read in three steps.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Identify the matrix, vector, quadratic form, Hessian, residual, or design matrix.</p></li>
        <li><p>Identify the spectral, curvature, or least-squares operation.</p></li>
        <li><p>Interpret what the result says about directions, local behavior, approximation, or fitted coefficients.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <table xml:id="tab-u5-reading-computations">
        <title>Reading Unit 5 computations</title>
        <tabular>
          <col width="30%"/>
          <col width="25%"/>
          <col width="35%"/>
          <row header="yes"><cell><p>Code fragment</p></cell><cell><p>First question to ask</p></cell><cell><p>Typical interpretation</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>np.linalg.eig(A)</c></p></cell><cell><p>Is <c>A</c> a square matrix?</p></cell><cell><p>Eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a general square matrix.</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>np.linalg.eigh(A)</c></p></cell><cell><p>Is <c>A</c> symmetric?</p></cell><cell><p>Eigenvalues and an orthonormal eigenvector basis.</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>Q.T @ A @ Q</c></p></cell><cell><p>Are the columns of <c>Q</c> orthonormal eigenvectors?</p></cell><cell><p>Orthogonal diagonalization and principal-axis coordinates.</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>x @ (A @ x)</c></p></cell><cell><p>What symmetric matrix defines the rule?</p></cell><cell><p>Value of the quadratic form <m>\mathbf x^TA\mathbf x</m>.</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>np.linalg.eigvalsh(H)</c></p></cell><cell><p>Is <c>H</c> the Hessian at a critical point?</p></cell><cell><p>Curvature signs for the second derivative test.</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>np.linalg.lstsq(A, b,</c> <c>rcond=None)[0]</c></p></cell><cell><p>What do the columns of <c>A</c> represent?</p></cell><cell><p>A least-squares coefficient vector.</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>A.T @ r</c></p></cell><cell><p>How was the residual defined?</p></cell><cell><p>Residual orthogonality; equivalently, a zero-gradient check.</p></cell></row>
          <row><cell><p><c>np.column_stack([...])</c></p></cell><cell><p>Which fixed features are being collected?</p></cell><cell><p>A fixed-feature design matrix for least squares.</p></cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="sec-u5-review-activities">
      <title>Review activities</title>
      <activity>
        <title>Reading Hessian eigenvalues</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Suppose a critical point has Hessian matrix</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[H = np.array([[4.0, 0.0],
              [0.0, -1.0]])

evals, evecs = np.linalg.eig(H)
evals
]]></code></program>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[array([ 4., -1.])
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What matrix is being studied?</p></li>
            <li><p>What do the signs of the eigenvalues say about the quadratic form <m>\mathbf{h}^T H\mathbf{h}</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>If <m>H</m> is the Hessian at a critical point, what does the second derivative test conclude?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why is the phrase <q>at a critical point</q> important?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO4, U5-LO5 | C+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The matrix being studied is the Hessian matrix. Its eigenvalues have opposite signs, so the quadratic form <m>\mathbf{h}^T H\mathbf{h}</m> takes both positive and negative values. If <m>H</m> is the Hessian at a critical point, the second derivative test classifies the critical point as a saddle point. The phrase <q>at a critical point</q> is important because the Hessian test classifies local behavior after the first-order term has disappeared.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity>
        <title>Reading a least-squares residual check</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Consider the code:</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[xhat = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]
r = b - A @ xhat
A.T @ r
]]></code></program>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What is <c>xhat</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is <c>r</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What condition is checked by <c>A.T @ r</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Does <c>A.T @ r</c> being <q>close to zero</q> mean that <c>r</c> is <q>close to zero</q>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which earlier unit used this same condition?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO6, U4-LO4 | C+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The vector <c>xhat</c> is a least-squares coefficient vector. The vector <c>r</c> is the residual
            <me>
              \mathbf{r}=\mathbf{b}-A\hat{\mathbf{x}}.
            </me>
            The expression <c>A.T @ r</c> checks whether the residual is orthogonal to the columns of <m>A</m>. It can be close to zero even when <c>r</c> is not close to zero. Unit 4 used this same residual-orthogonality condition to derive the normal equations. Unit 5 obtains the same equations by setting the gradient of <m>\|A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}\|^2</m> equal to zero.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity xml:id="act-u5-recap-spectral-tools">
        <title>Reading spectral tools</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Consider the code:</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A = np.array([[2.0, 1.0],
              [1.0, 2.0]])

evals, Q = np.linalg.eigh(A)
D = Q.T @ A @ Q
x = np.array([1.0, -1.0])
q = x @ (A @ x)
]]></code></program>
          <p>One possible output is</p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\texttt{evals}\amp=\begin{bmatrix}1\\3\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>Q\amp=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\begin{bmatrix}-1\amp1\\1\amp1\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>D=Q^TAQ\amp=\begin{bmatrix}1\amp0\\0\amp3\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>q=\mathbf x^TA\mathbf x\amp=2.</mrow>
          </md>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What do the entries of <c>evals</c> and the columns of <c>Q</c> represent?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does <c>Q.T @ A @ Q</c> give a diagonal matrix?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>x @ (A @ x)</c> compute?</p></li>
            <li><p>Use the eigenvalues to classify the quadratic form <m>q_A(\mathbf x)=\mathbf x^TA\mathbf x</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO1, U5-LO2, U5-LO3, U5-LO4 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The entries of <c>evals</c> are the eigenvalues <m>1</m> and <m>3</m>. The corresponding columns of <c>Q</c> are orthonormal eigenvectors. NumPy may return either sign for either eigenvector, so the signs of the displayed columns may change without changing the mathematics.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because <m>A</m> is symmetric, its orthonormal eigenvectors form the columns of an orthogonal matrix <m>Q</m>, and <m>Q^TAQ</m> is the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues. The expression <c>x @ (A @ x)</c> computes the quadratic form <m>q_A(\mathbf x)=\mathbf x^TA\mathbf x</m>, which equals <m>2</m> for the displayed vector. Both eigenvalues are positive, so <m>q_A</m> is positive definite.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <p xml:id="sec-u5-review-activities-4">
        Reopen <xref ref="sec-u5-code-fixed-hidden-layer" text="custom">The same fixed-feature fit in code</xref> for the fixed-feature design-matrix calculation.
      </p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="lab-u5-callout">
      <title>Linked notebook</title>
      <p>
        <em>Linked notebook.</em> Run <url href="https://sebroc.github.io/MATH345-Course-Materials/labs/#lab-u5">Lab U5: Hessian eigenvalues, least squares, and fixed nonlinear features</url>. The opening block reviews gradient descent and a final-layer update from Unit 3. The Unit 5 core interprets Hessian eigenvalues, checks least squares through residual and gradient conditions, distinguishes global minimality from coefficient uniqueness, and fits fixed nonlinear features.
      </p>
    </subsection>
    <conclusion>
    <p>
      For a quick reference on entrywise functions, design matrices, eigenvalue and least-squares commands, transposes, and numerical checks such as <q>close to zero</q>, see the programming appendix sections <xref ref="appcomp-elementwise-vs-linear-algebra" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-array-constructors" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-numpy-linalg" text="global"/>, and <xref ref="appcomp-numerical-checks" text="global"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Exam skill.</em> Given a short Unit 5 calculation, code fragment, table, or matrix output, identify whether it concerns an eigenpair, diagonalization, quadratic form, Hessian classification, least-squares solve, residual check, or fixed-feature design matrix, and interpret the result. <em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO1, U5-LO2, U5-LO3, U5-LO4, U5-LO5, U5-LO6, U5-LO7 | C+M+T | Core]</c>
    </p>
    </conclusion>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u5-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Exercise tags.</em> The tags below identify the primary outcomes and competency types for each exercise group. Individual problems may also involve earlier outcomes.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="u5-exercises-sec-orthogonal-diagonalization">
      <title>Symmetric matrices and orthogonal diagonalization</title>
      <p><em>Primary outcomes.</em> U5-LO3. <em>Competencies.</em> P+C.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/08%3A_Orthogonality/8.02%3A_Orthogonal_Diagonalization/8.2E%3A_Orthogonal_Diagonalization_Exercises">Nicholson 8.2.5(b,d,f)</url></p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u5-exercises-sec-second-derivative-test-and-saddle-points">
      <title>Hessians and critical-point classification</title>
      <p><em>Primary outcomes.</em> U3-LO9, U5-LO5. <em>Competencies.</em> P+C.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><url href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-7-maxima-minima-problems">OpenStax 4.7 #319</url></p></li>
        <li><p><url href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-7-maxima-minima-problems">OpenStax 4.7 #323</url></p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u5-exercises-applied-computational">
      <title>Additional applied and computational problems</title>
      <p>
        These exercises connect eigenvectors, quadratic forms, Hessian eigenvalues, least squares, residual checks, fixed nonlinear features, and code interpretation. Solutions are collected in Appendix <xref ref="app-u5-applied-solutions" text="global"/>.
      </p>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u5-eigenvectors-special-directions">
        <title>Eigenvectors as special directions</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              3\amp1\\
              0\amp2
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{v}_1=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\\
              0
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{v}_2=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              -1\\
              1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{v}_1</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{v}_2</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Identify the eigenvalue for each eigenvector.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain what the matrix map does to these two special directions.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO1 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u5-diagonalization-matrix-powers">
        <title>Diagonalization and matrix powers</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              2\amp1\\
              0\amp3
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              P=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\amp1\\
              0\amp1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              D=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              2\amp0\\
              0\amp3
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Verify that the columns of <m>P</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, and identify their eigenvalues.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>P^{-1}</m> and verify that <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use <m>A^k=PD^kP^{-1}</m> to derive a formula for <m>A^k</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>A^4</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain why diagonalization makes repeated matrix actions easier to compute.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO1, U5-LO2 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u5-quadratic-form-definiteness">
        <title>Quadratic form and definiteness</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              H=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              4\amp0\\
              0\amp1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute <m>\mathbf{h}^T H\mathbf{h}</m> for
                <me>
                  \mathbf{h}=
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                  1\\
                  0
                  \end{bmatrix},
                  \qquad
                  \mathbf{h}=
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                  0\\
                  1
                  \end{bmatrix},
                  \qquad
                  \mathbf{h}=
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                  1\\
                  1
                  \end{bmatrix}.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Write <m>\mathbf{h}^T H\mathbf{h}</m> for a general vector
                <me>
                  \mathbf{h}=
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                  h_1\\
                  h_2
                  \end{bmatrix}.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Is <m>H</m> positive definite, negative definite, or indefinite?</p></li>
            <li><p>If <m>H</m> is the Hessian at a critical point, what does the second derivative test conclude?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO4, U5-LO5 | P+C | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u5-hessian-eigenvalue-classification">
        <title>Hessian eigenvalue classification</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is a critical point of a scalar-valued function <m>f</m>. For each possible list of Hessian eigenvalues, classify the critical point as a local minimum, local maximum, saddle point, or inconclusive.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p><m>3,5</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>-2,-7</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>4,-1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>0,2</m></p></li>
            <li><p>Explain why the zero eigenvalue case is different.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO5 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u5-least-squares-from-gradients">
        <title>Least squares from gradients</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\amp0\\
              1\amp1\\
              1\amp2
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{b}=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\\
              2\\
              2
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Define
            <me>h(\mathbf{x})=\|A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}\|^2.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A^T A</m> and <m>A^T\mathbf{b}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use <m>\nabla h(\mathbf{x})=2A^T(A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b})</m> to write the normal equations.</p></li>
            <li><p>Solve the normal equations.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute the residual <m>\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{b}-A\hat{\mathbf{x}}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check that <m>A^T\mathbf{r}=\mathbf{0}</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO6, U4-LO4 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u5-residual-orthogonality-code">
        <title>Residual orthogonality in code</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Consider the code:</p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[xhat = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]
r = b - A @ xhat
A.T @ r
]]></code></program>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>What is <c>xhat</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is <c>r</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What condition is checked by <c>A.T @ r</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Does <c>A.T @ r</c> being close to zero mean that <c>r</c> is close to zero?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which earlier unit used this same condition?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO6, U4-LO4 | C+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u5-fixed-hidden-layer-design-matrix">
        <title>Fixed-feature design matrix</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>\sigma(t)=\tanh(t)</m>, and define
            <me>N_{\mathbf{c}}(t)=c_0+c_1\sigma(t)+c_2\sigma(t-1)+c_3\sigma(t+1).</me>
            Use the input values <m>t=-1,0,1</m>.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Write the design matrix <m>A</m> for the model <m>A\mathbf{c}\approx \mathbf{y}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which vector is trained?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why is this a least-squares problem?</p></li>
            <li><p>Is the model linear as a function of <m>t</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Is the model linear as a function of <m>\mathbf{c}</m>?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U5-LO6, U5-LO7 | C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u5-highlights">
    <title>Unit 5 highlights</title>
    <p><em>Big questions.</em> Which directions make a matrix simple? How do those directions describe quadratic forms and the local shape of a function? How do projection, zero gradient, and curvature describe the same least-squares problem?</p>
    <p><em>Notation.</em></p>
    <me>
      A\mathbf v=\lambda\mathbf v,\qquad
      A=PDP^{-1},\qquad
      A=QDQ^T,
    </me>
    <me>
      q_A(\mathbf x)=\mathbf x^TA\mathbf x,\qquad
      H_f(\mathbf a),\qquad
      L(\mathbf x)=\|A\mathbf x-\mathbf b\|^2,
    </me>
    <me>
      A^T\mathbf r=\mathbf0,\qquad
      N_{\mathbf c}(t)=\boldsymbol{\phi}(t)^T\mathbf c,\qquad
      L(\mathbf c)=\|A\mathbf c-\mathbf y\|^2.
    </me>
    <p><em>Concepts.</em> Eigenvalue, eigenvector, eigenspace, invariant line, diagonalization, orthogonal matrix, orthogonal diagonalization, spectral theorem, principal axes, quadratic form, positive definite matrix, negative definite matrix, indefinite matrix, Hessian, quadratic approximation, saddle point, least-squares loss, residual, fixed feature, design matrix, and trained coefficient.</p>
    <p><em>Results.</em> Eigenvectors identify lines preserved by a matrix map. A matrix is diagonalizable exactly when it has a basis of eigenvectors. Symmetric matrices have an orthonormal eigenvector basis. In principal-axis coordinates, a quadratic form is a sum of scaled squares, so eigenvalue signs determine definiteness.</p>
    <p>At a critical point, Hessian eigenvalue signs classify second-order local behavior: all positive gives a local minimum, all negative gives a local maximum, and mixed signs give a saddle point. A zero eigenvalue makes the test inconclusive.</p>
    <p>The least-squares normal equations express both residual orthogonality and zero gradient. If <m>\widehat{\mathbf x}</m> solves them, then</p>
    <me>
      L(\widehat{\mathbf x}+\mathbf z)
      =
      L(\widehat{\mathbf x})+\|A\mathbf z\|^2.
    </me>
    <p>Thus <m>\widehat{\mathbf x}</m> is a global minimizer, and null-space directions determine whether its coefficient vector is unique.</p>
    <p>A model may be nonlinear in its input while remaining linear in its fitted coefficients. With fixed nonlinear features and squared-error loss, fitting the coefficient vector is an ordinary least-squares problem.</p>
    <p><em>Toolbox skills.</em> Find eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and eigenspaces; test diagonalizability; construct <m>PDP^{-1}</m> and <m>QDQ^T</m>; compute matrix powers; evaluate and diagonalize quadratic forms; classify definiteness; interpret Hessian eigenvalues; use the general and two-variable second derivative tests; derive normal equations; check <m>A^T\mathbf r=\mathbf0</m>; distinguish global minimality from coefficient uniqueness; build a fixed-feature design matrix; and read short spectral and least-squares code.</p>
    <p><em>Main applications.</em> Repeated matrix actions, principal directions of symmetric maps, quadratic-form geometry, local classification of critical points, least squares from projection and gradients, and fitting linear combinations of fixed nonlinear features.</p>
    <p><em>Connections.</em> <xref ref="u1-vectors-matrices-linear-maps" text="custom">Unit 1</xref> supplies matrix maps, products, transposes, and symmetric matrices. <xref ref="u2-anatomy-linear-map" text="custom">Unit 2</xref> supplies null spaces, rank, invertibility, and nonunique coefficient descriptions. <xref ref="u3-beyond-linear-maps" text="custom">Unit 3</xref> supplies critical points, gradients, Hessians, quadratic local approximation, and gradient descent. <xref ref="u4-orthogonality-least-squares" text="custom">Unit 4</xref> supplies orthogonality, projection, residuals, design matrices, and least squares. Unit 6 adds equality constraints through Lagrange multipliers and reuses symmetric quadratic forms in problems about maximum stretch, PCA, and SVD.</p>
    <p><em>Study anchors.</em> U5-LO1: <xref ref="sec-eigenvalues-eigenvectors-diagonalization" text="custom">Eigenvalues and eigenvectors</xref>. U5-LO2: <xref ref="subsec-diagonalization" text="custom">Diagonalization</xref>. U5-LO3: <xref ref="sec-orthogonal-diagonalization" text="custom">Symmetric matrices and orthogonal diagonalization</xref>. U5-LO4: <xref ref="subsec-u5-quadratic-forms-definiteness" text="custom">Quadratic forms and definiteness</xref>. U5-LO5: <xref ref="sec-second-derivative-test-and-saddle-points" text="custom">Hessians and the second derivative test</xref>. U5-LO6: <xref ref="sec-least-squares-redux" text="custom">Least squares: three viewpoints</xref>. U5-LO7: <xref ref="sec-tiny-nn-training" text="custom">Least squares with fixed nonlinear features</xref> and <xref ref="sec-u5-apps-computation" text="custom">Applications and computation recap</xref>.</p>
    <p><em>Applications and computations readiness checklist.</em> Given a short Unit 5 calculation, code snippet, table, or diagram, I can compute and interpret an eigenpair; distinguish an eigenvalue from an eigenvector; decide whether enough eigenvectors exist for diagonalization; use <m>PDP^{-1}</m> to understand repeated matrix actions; interpret <m>QDQ^T</m> as orthogonal diagonalization; evaluate a quadratic form; classify definiteness from eigenvalue signs; use Hessian eigenvalues to classify a critical point; recognize a zero eigenvalue as inconclusive; use the two-variable discriminant as a shortcut; interpret <c>np.linalg.lstsq</c>; interpret <c>A.T @ r</c> as both residual orthogonality and a zero-gradient check; distinguish global minimality from uniqueness; identify fixed feature columns; write the squared-error loss; and identify the trained coefficient vector.</p>
    <p><em>Common mistakes.</em> Confusing eigenvalues with eigenvectors; treating the zero vector as an eigenvector; assuming every square matrix is diagonalizable; confusing <m>P^{-1}</m> with <m>P^T</m>; using orthogonal diagonalization for a nonsymmetric matrix; judging definiteness only from diagonal entries; applying the Hessian test away from a critical point; treating a zero Hessian eigenvalue as a classification; thinking <m>A^T\mathbf r=\mathbf0</m> means <m>\mathbf r=\mathbf0</m>; using a local Hessian test alone to claim a global least-squares minimum; assuming the coefficient vector is unique without checking column independence; saying that nonlinear features prevent least squares; or assuming one least-squares solve also trains parameters inside the feature functions.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
